<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Certificate pinning for release builds (brief §4/§5.2, R-MOBILE-2 in
  04_Threat_Model_Risk_Assessment.md). Debug builds use their own copy,
  src/debug/res/xml/network_security_config.xml, which also allows plain HTTP
  to localhost for the adb-reverse tunnel described in src/config.ts: with a
  network security config present, Android ignores the manifest's
  usesCleartextTraffic. Keep the pins in both files the same.

  Pins are REAL, recomputed against the live deployment on 2026-09-18. To
  reproduce, fetch the chain and split it per certificate:
    openssl s_client -connect d2zb1uxt99m5ks.cloudfront.net:443 \
      -servername d2zb1uxt99m5ks.cloudfront.net -showcerts </dev/null \
      2>/dev/null > chain.pem
    csplit -z -f cert- -b "%02d.pem" chain.pem '/BEGIN CERT/' '{*}'
  then hash each one's public key:
    openssl x509 -pubkey -noout -in certNN.pem | openssl pkey -pubin -outform der \
      | openssl dgst -sha256 -binary | openssl enc -base64

  Keep any PEM banner text out of this comment. XML forbids a double hyphen
  inside a comment, and a pasted "BEGIN CERTIFICATE" banner carries five of
  them — which makes this file invalid XML. That is not a hypothetical: it is
  exactly what broke the first release build of this app, and it fails at
  resource-compile time, so nothing catches it until someone actually builds
  a release. Hence the abbreviated 'BEGIN CERT' pattern above.

  WHAT HAPPENED ON 2026-09-18, AND WHY THE PIN SET CHANGED SHAPE

  The previous version of this file predicted the leaf would rotate around
  2026-09-09 and stated that the backup pin — the issuing intermediate,
  "Amazon RSA 2048 M01" — "should survive that rotation since AWS controls
  the whole issuance chain for this cert type," and that this was "what keeps
  the app working through the rotation instead of hard-locking out."

  That reasoning was wrong, and checking rather than trusting it is the only
  reason we know. The leaf did rotate, and AWS also moved issuance to a
  DIFFERENT intermediate: Amazon RSA 2048 M04, not M01. So BOTH pins were
  stale simultaneously. A release build shipped between the rotation and this
  fix would have been unable to reach the API at all — not degraded, not
  falling back to system trust, but hard-failing every TLS handshake. That is
  the classic certificate-pinning failure mode, and this project walked into
  it exactly as documented-but-unverified assumptions tend to.

  The lesson is in the pin set below. A CA operator rotating intermediates is
  routine and outside our control, so pinning only leaf + intermediate is
  fragile by construction. Amazon Root CA 1 is added as a third, last-resort
  pin: it is a materially weaker guarantee (any certificate issued anywhere
  under that root would satisfy it) but it expires in 2037 and survives both
  leaf and intermediate rotation. Pinning is worthless if it is switched off
  after the first self-inflicted outage, so the trade is deliberate — a
  slightly weaker pin that stays enabled beats a stronger one that gets
  removed in a panic.

  Re-verify before the pin-set expiration below. Verify by recomputing
  against the live host, never by assuming a rotation kept the chain intact.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <domain-config>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">d2zb1uxt99m5ks.cloudfront.net</domain>
        <pin-set expiration="2027-03-01">
            <!-- Primary — leaf certificate (CN=*.cloudfront.net), valid until
                 2027-03-10. Rotates without notice; expect to replace this. -->
            <pin digest="SHA-256">dExBlAvT1n9yGuq8/3TYV0mGSoCBmqDo+95Hxr2avEE=</pin>
            <!-- Backup — issuing intermediate (Amazon RSA 2048 M04), valid
                 until 2030-08-23. Note this is M04: the previous pin named
                 M01, and AWS switched intermediates during the September 2026
                 rotation. Treat this pin as mutable, not stable. -->
            <pin digest="SHA-256">G9LNNAql897egYsabashkzUCTEJkWBzgoEtk8X/678c=</pin>
            <!-- Last resort — Amazon Root CA 1, valid until 2037-12-31. Added
                 2026-09-18 after both pins above went stale at once. Weaker
                 (it trusts the whole root), but it is what prevents a routine
                 CA-side rotation from bricking the installed app. -->
            <pin digest="SHA-256">++MBgDH5WGvL9Bcn5Be30cRcL0f5O+NyoXuWtQdX1aI=</pin>
        </pin-set>
    </domain-config>

    <!-- Everything else falls through to the platform default: system trust
         store, no pinning, cleartext blocked. In debug builds the local-dev
         domains in src/config.ts get plain HTTP from the debug copy of this
         file. Pinning is deliberately scoped to the production API
         domain only — pinning localhost would be both meaningless (no real
         TLS cert exists there) and would break local development. -->
</network-security-config>
